{:edb/db-before-t 2107 :edb/db-after-t 2108 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535420 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:46:22.754Z" 13194139535420 true] [17592186083306 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348346 13194139535420 true] [17592186083307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348339 13194139535420 true] [17592186083510 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535420 true] [17592186083510 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. Given that it is at rest when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity (in $\\text{m/s}$)  of the particle at time $t.$" 13194139535420 true] [17592186083510 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $m/s^{2}$, given by the function $a(t) = \\frac{1}{2}\\cos (2t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. Given that it is at rest when $t = 0s$, find the velocity (in $m/s$) of the particle at time $t$." 13194139535420 false] [17592186083510 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$" 13194139535420 true] [17592186083827 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535420 true] [17592186083827 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2t-9{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535420 true] [17592186083827 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $m/s^{2}$, given by the function $a(t) = 2t - 9t^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v =-2m/s$ when $t = 1s$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t = 2s$." 13194139535420 false] [17592186083827 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-9{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =(1)^{2}-3(1)^{3}+C \\\\ -2 & =1-3+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(2)^{2}-3(2)^{3} \\\\  & =4-24 \\\\  & =-20\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $20\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535420 true] [17592186348339 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348340 13194139535420 true] [17592186348339 1089 "q-19489" 13194139535420 true] [17592186348339 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535420 true] [17592186348339 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139535420 true] [17592186348339 1113 #uuid "66dc2a76-d4e2-534c-99d2-6f4373844378" 13194139535420 true] [17592186348339 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4(-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{4}{5}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot 0)+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 1+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =-0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535420 true] [17592186348340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348341 13194139535420 true] [17592186348340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348342 13194139535420 true] [17592186348340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348343 13194139535420 true] [17592186348340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348344 13194139535420 true] [17592186348340 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348345 13194139535420 true] [17592186348340 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535420 true] [17592186348340 1041 "selection" 13194139535420 true] [17592186348340 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348343 13194139535420 true] [17592186348340 1110 #uuid "dd9d928a-ed3b-5f20-aa17-e6eb6628bba2" 13194139535420 true] [17592186348341 1034 "1.25\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139535420 true] [17592186348341 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535